    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently reviewing the stability profiles for a new low-fat mayonnaise formulation. We're seeing some issues with phase separation in high-acid environments (pH < 3.8). Can you explain the specific molecular role of lecithin in stabilizing the oil-water interface under these high proton concentrations, and how the amphiphilic nature of the molecule helps prevent coalescence?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Lecithin, a phospholipid component of the oil-water interface in mayonnaise, plays a crucial role in stabilizing the emulsion in high-acid environments. Its amphiphilic nature, consisting of a hydrophilic phosphate head and hydrophobic tails, allows it to anchor itself to the oil droplets, creating a hydrophobic coating that resists coalescence and maintains emulsion stability. In acidic conditions, where the negatively charged phosphate groups are maximally deprotonated, their hydrophilic character increases, enhancing the interaction with the aqueous phase and further stabilizing the interface. This prevents oil droplets from coalescing and maintains the overall emulsion structure.",
      "source": "target",
